          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological
            spectrum is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            in the universal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Sp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn ∞
                  %mi Grpd
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp(Top) \simeq Sp(\infty Grpd)
            that stabilizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-Grpd"} ∞-Grpd
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            under the operations of forming
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space+objects"} loop space objects
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+suspensions"} reduced suspensions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+of+spectra"} stable (∞,1)-category of spectra
          %p
            More generally, one may consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+objects"} spectrum objects
            in any
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentable (∞,1)-category
          %h3#connective_and_nonconnective_spectra_infinite_loop_spaces Connective and non-connective spectra; infinite loop spaces
          %p
            As opposed to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+a+spectrum"} homotopy groups of a spectrum
            are defined and may be non-trivial in
            %em negative
            integer degree. This follows from the fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
            operation is by construction invertible on spectra, which implies that for every spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            these and all
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-fold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega^n
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+groups"} stable homotopy groups
            given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mo −
                        %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi n
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi E
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{k-n}(\Omega^n E) \simeq \pi_k(E)
          %p
            Those spectra for which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+spectra"} homotopy groups of spectra
            in negative degree happen to vanish are called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+spectra"} connective spectra
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+loop+spaces"} infinite loop spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+spaces"} E-∞ spaces
          %p
            Connective spectra in the image of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            operation of the classical
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            that are not only connective spectra but even have a
            %em strict
            symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            structure with non-negatively graded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complexes
            of abelian groups.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Ch
                            %mo +
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi Dold
                              %mo −
                              %mi Kan
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi nerve
                        %mtd
                          %mi ConnectSp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Grp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊂
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Grpd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 2
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∂
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 1
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∂
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 0
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                          %mo →
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ↦
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Ch_+ &amp;\stackrel{Dold-Kan \; nerve}{\to}&amp;
                  ConnectSp(\infty Grp) \subset \infty Grpd
                  \\\
                  (\cdots A_2 \stackrel{\partial}{\to} A_1 \stackrel{\partial}{\to} A_0 \to 0 \to 0 \to \cdots)
                  \&amp;\stackrel{}{\mapsto}&amp;
                  N(A_\bullet)
                  }
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"} homology
            groups of the chain complex correspond precisely to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            of the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} ∞-groupoid
          %p
            The free stabilization of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of non-negatively graded chain complexes is simply the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            of arbitrary chain complexes. There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+Dold-Kan+correspondence"} stable Dold-Kan correspondence
            (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module+spectrum"} module spectrum
            the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "module+spectrum#StableDoldKanCorrespondence"} stable Dold-Kan correspondence
            ) that identifies these with special objects in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Top
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp(Top)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ch
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mrow
                              %mi Dold
                              %mo −
                              %mi Kan
                              %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                              %mi nerve
                        %mtd
                          %mi Sp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mi Grp
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ⋯
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 2
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∂
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 1
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∂
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mn 0
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∂
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mo ∂
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 2
                          %mo →
                          %mi ⋯
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo ↦
                            %mrow
                        %mtd
                          %mi N
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi A
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  Ch &amp;\stackrel{Dold-Kan \; nerve}{\to}&amp;
                  Sp(\infty Grp)
                  \\\
                  (\cdots A_2 \stackrel{\partial}{\to} A_1 \stackrel{\partial}{\to} A_0 \stackrel{\partial}{\to} A_{-1}
                  \\stackrel{\partial}{\to} A_{-2} \to \cdots)
                  \&amp;\stackrel{}{\mapsto}&amp;
                  N(A_\bullet)
                  }
          %p So it is the homologically nontrivial parts of the chain complexes in negative degree that corresponds to the non-connectiveness of a spectrum.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            There are many “models” for spectra, all of which present the same (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"}> stable
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            (and in fact, nearly all of them are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model categories
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+Homotopy+Theory"} Introduction to Stable Homotopy Theory
          %h3#SequentialPreSpectra Sequential pre-spectra
          %p
            A simple first definition is to define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-spectrum"} pre-spectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            to be a sequence of pointed spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (E_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}
            together with structure maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                  %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma{}E_n\to{}E_{n+1}
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            denotes the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+suspension"} reduced suspension
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+sequential+spectra"} model structure on sequential spectra
          %p
            There are various conditions that can be put on the spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n
            and the structure maps, for example if the spaces are CW-complexes and the structure maps are inclusions of subcomplexes, the spectrum is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-spectrum"} CW-spectrum
          %p
            Without any condition, this is just called a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong spectrum
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-spectrum"} pre-spectrum
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate-free+spectra"} coordinate-free spectra
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectra"} sequential spectra
          %p
            For details see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+1-1"} Introduction to stable homotopy theory – 1.1 Sequential Spectra
          %h3#OmegaSpectrum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            \-spectra
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+space"} loop space
            functor on the category of pointed spaces, we know that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            is left adjoint to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_n\to\Omega{}E_{n+1}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            is called an
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
                \-spectrum
          %p
            The idea is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_0
            contains the information of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            in dimensions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\ge 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_1
            contains the information of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo ≥
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\ge -1
            (but shifted up by one, so that it is modeled by the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ge 0
            information in the space
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_1
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            \-spectra are special cases of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectrum"} sequential
            pre-spectra as
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SequentialPreSpectra"} above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+spectra"} model structure on spectra
          %p
            Given any sequential pre-spectrum
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            = succeed "-spectrum" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{F}
            (a fibrant replacement of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{E}
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrification"} spectrification
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#LewisMaySteinberger86"} Lewis-May-Steinberger 86, p. 3
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo →
                      %mn ∞
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi m
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_n \coloneqq \lim_{m\to\infty}\Omega^m E_{n+m}
          %p
            (using that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            commutes with the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimits"} filtered colimits
          %h3#coordinatefree_spectra Coordinate-free spectra
          %p A definition of spectrum consisting of spaces indexed by index sets less “coordinatized” than the integers is a
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate-free+spectrum"}> coordinate-free spectrum
              \.
          %p See there for details.
          %h3#combinatorial_spectra Combinatorial spectra
          %p
            There might be a type of categorical structure related to a spectrum in the same way that
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-groupoids." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-cells" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
                \-category
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            One realization of this kind of idea is the notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+spectrum"} combinatorial spectrum
          %h3#general_context General context
          %p
            See
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum+object"} spectrum object
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+spectrum"} suspension spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+spectrum"} K-theory spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elliptic+spectrum"} elliptic spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tmf"} tmf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thom+spectrum"}> Thom spectrum
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+cobordism+spectrum"} complex cobordism spectrum
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#stabilization Stabilization
          %p
            In direct analogy to how topological spaces form the archetypical example,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Top
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sp(Top)
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} stable (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+category"} pointed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            into a stable
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sp
